-The generated records help the classifiers that fail on the minority class and slightly harm those that are already strong, so their effect depends on the dataset and the classifier. Classifiers trained on real data alone already vary widely: on the 195-record Parkinson's dataset, MLP accuracy is $72.3\pm18.2\%$ while tree ensembles exceed 92\%, and on heart failure SVM and $k$NN reach over 60\% accuracy with an F1 of almost zero because they rarely predict the minority class. With the reference configuration, F1 improves in 26 of the 59 classifier--dataset combinations and decreases in 31 (Figure~\ref{fig:heatmap}); the largest gains occur where real-only training fails, such as $k$NN on heart failure ($+15.3$ F1 points) and MLP on Parkinson's ($+8.2$ accuracy points). No single combination is significant after Holm correction, and the change varies widely between divisions. On average, F1 is unchanged with GPT-4o mini ($-0.14$ pp) and increases by 1.09 pp with GPT-4.1, which is consistent with the bound set by information theory: synthetic records cannot carry more information about the task than the real records and the generator's knowledge together~\cite[Sec.~2.8]{cover2006elements}.
